[story]
# Story

//identity: Unclear flows needed a friendlier voice, so I created a pixel mascot that helps people from login through placing an order, then led the illustrations and animations that carried that voice through the product.
//customer-app: The React Native MVP made payments from external shops simple, with KYC that felt as easy as a normal checkout. The architecture was designed so the product could grow into a full marketplace.
//web: The merchant platform could work as a payment method or as the shop itself. We started with existing shops — products, billing, and a to-do onboarding — so they could go live while heavier catalog flows were still in progress.

[overview]
# Overview

[open-banking]
# Open Banking & KYC

//problem: Open Banking and KYC flows varied significantly across banks. API responses were unpredictable, and some banks redirected users to external apps or websites.
//research: I analyzed regulatory requirements, bank documentation, API responses, and possible fallback scenarios.
//iterations: We designed a flexible interface framework that could adapt to anything from a single input to 20+ fields and handle external redirects consistently.
//outcome: A scalable KYC and payment flow that made Open Banking feel as simple and familiar as adding a card.

[marketplace]
# Marketplace Architecture

//problem: The MVP focused on payments, but the product needed to evolve into a full-cycle marketplace without a major redesign.
//research: We mapped the future customer journey, marketplace features, and dependencies between payments, products, orders, and merchant tools.
//iterations: We designed the navigation and product architecture to support both the initial payment-focused MVP and future online shopping scenarios.
//outcome: A scalable foundation that allowed Picksell to launch quickly and expand into a marketplace as the product gained traction.

[product]
# Product and Variant Management

//problem: Traditional merchant tools often force sellers to create separate, disconnected product entries for every size, color, or variation.
//research: We analyzed common catalog-management patterns and the difficulties merchants faced when updating large numbers of duplicated products.
//iterations: I designed a component-based product editor where one master product contained all its variants, while still supporting CSV imports.
//outcome: A single source of truth that made catalogs faster to create, easier to maintain, and less prone to inconsistencies.

[design-system]
# Cross-Platform Design System

//problem: Six designers were working across three platforms and more than 1,000 screens, creating a high risk of inconsistency and duplicated work.
//research: We audited shared patterns, platform-specific requirements, accessibility standards, and technical constraints.
//iterations: We built shared components, page-level templates, dedicated libraries, and a unified illustration and motion system.
//outcome: A single source of truth across all platforms, including 1,500+ design elements and 150+ illustrations and animations, fully aligned with WCAG standards.
